WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 98 for July 10th, 2026.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.940
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.640
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.020
Let's get into it. You know that feeling when an idea pops into your head and you wish someone

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:27.160
could just build it for you on the spot. A teenager recently described a brutalist city builder game

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:33.580
in plain words, and the latest GPT 5.6 model turned that description into a complete working

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:39.760
website. The site runs right in your browser with traffic moving, day turning tonight, and modes

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.940
you can unlock. Think of it like asking a super fast architect to sketch an entire interactive toy

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:52.360
from one sentence, then hand it over ready to play. Ethan Mollick tested this by giving the model

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:58.040
a simple request to recreate something he had built earlier with older tools. This time the

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:04.420
model handled every detail inside a coding helper called Codex without any extra human fixes.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.760
The result feels like a small video game that keeps running on its own, complete with an

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.240
achievement system and lighting changes. What stands out is how much smoother the output became

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.720
in just a few months. Older versions needed someone to adjust the code afterward, but this one works

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.660
from the first try. If you have ever sketched cities in a notebook or built things in Minecraft during

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.960
study breaks, this shows the gap between an idea and a playable version is getting smaller.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.980
You might soon turn a random thought into a quick interactive tool without learning any programming

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:46.220
steps. Right now you can find the demo Ethan shared and open it on a laptop or phone. Try clicking

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.300
the different visual modes in the corner or slowing the simulation down to watch how the lights and

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.400
traffic shift. It is a zero pressure way to see what the model can already create from words alone.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.180
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:09.100
The part that feels like magic here is how the model turns a short request into working buttons

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.920
and moving graphics. Start with something familiar, like the way your phone guesses the next word while

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:21.320
you text. The same idea scales up, except instead of one word, it predicts whole blocks of instructions

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:27.300
that make a website function. It has studied millions of existing code examples the way a musician

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:32.460
studies songs, noticing repeating patterns like what happens when a button is clicked.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:38.000
From there, it assembles new instructions that follow those patterns, but match your exact

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.620
request. Step by step, it adds the layout, connects the day night cycle, and drops in an achievement list.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:51.340
The model is not copying any single older game. It is combining pieces it has seen into something fresh.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.740
By the end, the whole experience runs because the patterns lined up with what you asked for.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:02.780
That is why the city appeared so quickly and already included extra modes like cyberpunk lighting.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.660
Once you see the process as fast pattern matching turned into clickable parts, it stops feeling

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:14.780
mysterious and starts feeling like a very clever recipe. Let's move to something practical you can

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:21.900
do today. Instagram recently added a tool called Muse AI that can pull photos from public accounts to

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.960
create new pictures. If your profile is open, someone else can tag you and the system will mix your

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:33.940
images into whatever scene they describe. Think of it like leaving your photo album on a table where

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:40.140
anyone can borrow pages without telling you. To stop that, open the app, tap your profile picture,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:46.320
then the three lines at the top right. Scroll to the section called sharing and reuse and switch

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.560
both posts and reels to off. The whole change takes less than a minute and keeps your pictures from

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.800
being reused without your say. It is a small step that gives you control over how your images travel

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:05.280
online. Here is another hands-on experience worth knowing about. A new gallery called Data Land

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:11.560
opened with art made entirely by AI systems. Visitors wear simple sensors so the pictures and

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.220
sculptures shift in real time with their heartbeat and breathing. Picture walking through a room where

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.600
the walls respond to you the way a plant might lean towards sunlight. The space mixes natural materials

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:29.720
with technology to show how the two can work together instead of competing. If you have only seen AI

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:35.620
pictures sitting flat on a screen, this gives a different sense of what the tools can do when they

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:42.020
react to a real person. Plans for visits are shared on the wired story if you want to check whether the

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:50.100
gallery is near you. Quick bits now. Open AI posted short clips of real people already using GPT 5.6

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:56.980
in daily life. One shows a serial business run from a dining room table. Another follows a broccoli

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.160
farmer checking crop plans and a third features a mathematician solving tough problems.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:08.240
The examples make clear the same model is moving from labs straight into kitchens and fields.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.660
Scientists also built a robotic heart that copies the exact stages of a common heart condition.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:21.180
Researchers contest new treatments on the device first which speeds up safe experiments before any

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.940
human trials begin. It is a quiet example of AI and robotics helping medicine move forward without

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:34.860
extra risk. That's it for today. Remember every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.860
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.780
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch than listen,

